Title:
HAAG Storage & Semester-to-Semester Documentation Handoff Initiative
Initiative Questions:
Describe your initiative/ Procedure.
This initiative standardizes project storage and documentation handoff from semester to semester using GitHub as the source of truth. The core procedure is a simple repository organization that separates “authoritative” documentation from work-in-progress and historical materials, reducing lost context and improving continuity across semesters.
Procedure (current standard):

GitHub is the single source of truth for documentation.
Final documentation lives in final/.
Draft documentation lives in drafts/.
Archived (prior semester / outdated) documentation lives in archive/.
A consistent structure and naming convention supports fast retrieval and clear version trust.

Folder structure (defined):

final/ — approved, current docs for ongoing and future semesters
drafts/ — in-progress docs not yet validated
archive/ — old versions and prior-semester snapshots (reference only)
(Recommended additions for clarity: templates/, meeting-notes/, project-admin/)

Explain the hypotheses/ KPIs you have measured at this time and what is left to be measured.
Hypotheses:

A clear GitHub folder standard (final/drafts/archive) reduces time spent searching for documentation and reduces confusion about “what’s current.”
A consistent handoff structure improves continuity across semesters (less rework, fewer repeated decisions).
New members onboard faster with a stable “Start here” path and predictable artifact locations.

KPIs (planned):

Time-to-find key docs (minutes) for a new/returning member
Onboarding time to first productive contribution (hours/days)
Handoff completeness rate (% of required artifacts present in final/)
Number of clarification pings (e.g., “where is X?”) during onboarding
Adoption rate (% of project artifacts stored according to the standard)

Measured at this time:

Not yet measured / not provided.

Left to be measured:

Baseline vs. post-standard comparison for time-to-find and onboarding
End-of-semester completeness audit of final/
Short stakeholder pulse feedback after the structure is used during delivery

Explain your method for testing these hypotheses via flowcharts:
Test method (flowchart-ready steps):
Flowchart A — Discovery → Baseline

Identify current documentation locations and formats
Observe how documentation is found during normal work
Record common failure points (missing docs, duplicates, unclear “latest”)
Capture baseline: time-to-find + common clarification questions

Flowchart B — Implement → Pilot

Implement GitHub standard (final/, drafts/, archive/)
Migrate or classify existing docs into correct folders
Add minimal navigation (e.g., final/00_start-here/ landing doc)
Run a pilot across one or more deliverable cycles

Flowchart C — Measure → Iterate

Re-test time-to-find + onboarding experience
Check completeness of required artifacts in final/
Collect feedback (friction points, confusion, resistance)
Update structure/rules/templates based on results

